'''Margaret Wooster Curti''' (February 8, 1892 – September 19, 1961) was an American child psychologist, researcher and author.

Curti nee Wooster was born in [[Silver Creek, Nebraska]] on February 8, 1892.<ref name="Feminist Voices">{{cite web |title=Margaret Wooster Curti |url=https://feministvoices.com/profiles/margaret-wooster-curti |website=Feminist Voices |access-date=26 June 2025 |language=en}}</ref> She attended the [[University of Nebraska–Lincoln|University of Nebraska]] where she studied inder [[Harry Kirke Wolfe]], and the [[University of Chicago]] where she studied under [[Harvey A. Carr]], earning her PhD in 1920.<ref name="ANB">{{cite web |last1=Harris |first1=Benjamin |last2=Curti |first2=Merle |title=Curti, Margaret Wooster |url=https://doi.org/10.1093/anb/9780198606697.article.1400872 |website=American National Biography |access-date=26 June 2025 |language=en |doi=10.1093/anb/9780198606697.article.1400872}}</ref> In 1925 she married fellow academic [[Merle Curti]] with whom she had two children.

She taught at [[Smith College]] from 1922 through 1937, [[Teachers College, Columbia University]] from 1937 through 1942, and then briefly at [[University of Wisconsin–Madison]].<ref name="ANB"/>

In 1930 [[Longman|Longmans, Green & Co.]] published her book ''Child psychology''.<ref name="HathiTrust">{{cite web |last1=Curti |first1=Margaret Wooster |title=Child psychology |url=https://catalog.hathitrust.org/Record/005765389 |website=HathiTrust |publisher=Longmans, Green and co. |access-date=2025-06-28 |archive-date=2024-07-09 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240709141748/https://catalog.hathitrust.org/Record/005765389 |url-status=live }}</ref>

Curti researched [[cultural bias]] in testing, disputing the idea that race or economic class effect a child's intelligence.<ref name="ANB"/>

Curti died in Madison, Wisconsin on September 19, 1961.<ref name="Smith College Special Collections">{{cite web |title=Margaret Wooster Curti papers |url=https://findingaids.smith.edu/repositories/2/resources/1032 |website=Smith College Special Collections |access-date=26 June 2025 |archive-date=12 September 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240912213849/https://findingaids.smith.edu/repositories/2/resources/1032 |url-status=live }}</ref>

Her papers are in the [[Sophia Smith Collection]] at Smith College.<ref name="Smith College Special Collections"/>

